Plain sacks appear to have one "advantage" over oilskin sacks:
They are more common and so a player is likely to encounter
a plain sack first.

Plain sack is a practice item before you get an oilskin sack.
It's advantages (not exploding) make it worth carrying one if
you have one right up to the point you see your first oilskin
sack.  I suspect you can grease a plain sack and get a
temporary effect that matches the oilskin's permanent
advantage.  After you find that first oilskin sack, the plain one
can be ditched and never look back.

This isn't the only example of a more common item being a
practice run for a later one.  Among artifacts, Sting and
Magicbane follow the same pattern.

So the container strategy seems to be -

Carry a blessed bag of holding for most of the items that you
want ready access but don't need to be in your main inventory
at all times.  *Very* few items really need to stay in your
main inventory so this is more a question of what to carry to
stay unburdened.

Carry an oilskin sack for the few types of items that must not
go into a bag of holding.  Plain greased sack is the alternate
until you get the oilskin sack.

Use iceboxes for your hoards.  Boxes and chests are the
backup and the advantage of the icebox is so trivial it's hardly
worth bothering.  So you can include corpses in your hoards ...



